\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A witness group with two scalar orbits}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{2T} be the group of #{24} Hurwitz units, with the matrix
#{M} of \ref{fdip-00M6}, and let #{\omega=(-1+\qi+\qj+\qk)/2}. By
\ref{fdip-00QJ}, #{\omega^3=1}. Let #{\gamma} be the complex root
of #{t^2-t+1} with positive imaginary part,
#{\gamma=(1+\ui\sqrt{3})/2}, and #{|\gamma|^2=1}. Then
#{\gamma^2={\gamma}-1}, and
##{\gamma^3=\gamma(\gamma-1)=\gamma^2-\gamma=-1,\qquad \gamma^6=1.}
Thus #{\gamma} has order #{6}. Let #{C_6=\langle\gamma\rangle}. On
the product #{2T\times C_6}, identify #{\bigl(-1,\gamma^3\bigr)}
with the identity. The element #{-1} is central in the Hurwitz
units, and #{C_6} is abelian, so the identified element is central.
Call the quotient #{\Gew}. Since #{|2T\times C_6|=144} and the
identified subgroup has order #{2}, the quotient has order #{72}.
Write #{\,[u,m]\,} for the class of #{(u,\gamma^m)}. The product
sends #{\,[u,m]\,} to the matrix #{\gamma^m M(u)}. This is a
homomorphism into #{U(2)}, because #{\gamma^m} is scalar and
\ref{fdip-007G} gives #{\det M(u)=|u|^2} and
#{M(u)^{\dagger}M(u)=|u|^2\bbI}. Every Hurwitz unit has
norm #{1}, so #{M(u)} is special unitary and #{\gamma^m M(u)} is
unitary.}

\p{The homomorphism is faithful on #{\Gew}. If
#{\gamma^m M(u)} is the identity, the first column of
\ref{fdip-00M6} gives
#{(a+b\ui,-c+d\ui)=(\gamma^{-m},0)}. Hence #{c=d=0} and
#{a+b\ui=\gamma^{-m}}. By \ref{fgap-000K} the Hurwitz units are
#{8} units #{\pm1,\pm\qi,\pm\qj,\pm\qk} and #{16} units with
all #{4} coordinates in #{\{\pm1/2\}}. Vanishing of the last two
coordinates leaves #{\{\pm1,\pm\qi\}}. Their complex values
#{a+b\ui} are #{\pm1} and #{\pm\ui}. A power of #{\gamma} cannot
equal #{\pm\ui}: its square would be #{-1=\gamma^3}, so twice the
exponent would be congruent to #{3} modulo #{6}. The remaining
cases are #{u=1}, #{m=0}, and #{u=-1}, #{m=3}. Those two product
elements are the identity of #{\Gew}. The same first-column
equation shows that only the identity of #{\Gew} fixes
#{e_1=(1,0)}. The stabilizer of #{e_1} is trivial, so the #{72}
group elements send #{e_1} to distinct vectors.}

\p{Let #{h=[\omega,4]}, and let #{\beta=(\sqrt{3}-1)/2},
#{\alpha=\beta(1+\ui)} and #{v=(1,\alpha)}. The matrix of
#{\omega} is
##{M(\omega)=\begin{pmatrix}p&r\\ p&-r\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 p=\frac{-1+\ui}{2},\quad r=\frac{1+\ui}{2}.}
The first entry of #{M(\omega)v} is
##{p+r\alpha=\frac{-1+\ui}{2}+\ui\beta
 =-\frac12+\ui\Bigl(\frac12+\beta\Bigr)
 =-\frac12+\ui\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=\gamma^2,}
and the second entry is
##{p-r\alpha=-\frac12+\ui\Bigl(\frac12-\beta\Bigr)
 =-\frac12+\ui\frac{2-\sqrt{3}}{2}.}
The same value is #{\gamma^2\alpha}, because
#{\gamma^2(1+\ui)=-(1+\sqrt{3})/2+\ui(\sqrt{3}-1)/2} and
multiplication by #{\beta} gives #{-1/2} in the real part and
#{\beta^2=(2-\sqrt{3})/2} in the imaginary part. Thus
#{M(\omega)v=\gamma^2 v}, and
#{\gamma^4 M(\omega)v=\gamma^6 v=v}. The element #{h} fixes
#{v}. Also #{h^3=[\omega^3,12]=[1,0]}, while #{h} and
#{h^2=[\omega^2,2]} are distinct from the identity because
#{\omega} and #{\omega^2} lie outside #{\{\pm1\}}. The
stabilizer of #{v} contains this cyclic group of order three.}

\proof{
\p{Suppose #{\gamma^m M(u)} fixes #{v}, and write
#{u=a+b\qi+c\qj+d\qk}. First let #{u} be one of the #{8} units
#{\pm1,\pm\qi,\pm\qj,\pm\qk}. Scalar matrices are the classes of
#{\pm1}. The equation #{\gamma^m v=v} forces #{m=0}. The equation
#{\gamma^m(-v)=v} forces #{\gamma^m=-1}, hence #{m=3}, and
#{[-1,3]} is the identity of #{\Gew}. For #{\qi},
#{M(\qi)v=(\ui,-\ui\alpha)} is a scalar multiple of #{v} only if
#{\ui=-\ui}. For #{\qj},
#{M(\qj)v=(\alpha,-1)} is a scalar multiple of #{v} only if
#{\alpha^2=-1}. But #{\alpha^2=\ui(2-\sqrt{3})}, whose real part
is #{0}. For #{\qk}, #{M(\qk)v=(\ui\alpha,\ui)} forces
#{\alpha^2=1}, and the same square is not #{1}. Replacing the
unit by its negative multiplies the matrix by #{-1} and still
asks for a scalar multiple. Thus the only Lipschitz class that
fixes #{v} is the identity.}

\p{Now let every coordinate of #{u} lie in #{\{\pm1/2\}}. Write
#{z=a+b\ui} and #{w=c+d\ui}, and set #{\mu=c-d} and
#{\nu=c+d}. Then #{\mu} and #{\nu} lie in
#{\{-1,0,1\}}, one of them is #{0}, and the other is #{\pm1},
because their sum is #{\pm1}. The first row of
#{M(u)v=\lambda v}, with #{\lambda=\gamma^{-m}}, reads
##{z+w\alpha=(a+\beta\mu)+\ui(b+\beta\nu)=\lambda.}
If #{\mu=\pm1}, then #{\nu=0} and the imaginary part
of #{\lambda} equals #{b\in\{\pm1/2\}}. Every power of #{\gamma}
has imaginary part #{0} or #{\pm\sqrt{3}/2}. These differ from
#{\pm1/2} because #{\sqrt{3}\ne 1}. So #{\mu=0} and
#{\nu=\pm1}, and the real part of #{\lambda} is
#{a\in\{\pm1/2\}}. The possible powers are #{\gamma},
#{\gamma^2}, #{\gamma^4} and #{\gamma^5}. The opposite sign of
#{\nu} gives the coefficient #{\frac{2\sqrt{3}-1}{2}} or
#{\frac{1-2\sqrt{3}}{2}}. Neither lies in #{\{\pm1/2\}}:
equality would force #{\sqrt{3}=1} or #{\sqrt{3}=0}.}

\p{For #{\lambda=\gamma}, the choice #{\nu=1} gives #{b=1/2},
hence #{u=(1+\qi+\qj+\qk)/2=-\omega^2} and
#{\gamma^m=\gamma^5}. The class #{[-\omega^2,5]} equals
#{[\omega^2,2]=h^2}. For #{\lambda=\gamma^5}, the choice
#{\nu=-1} gives #{u=(1-\qi-\qj-\qk)/2=-\omega} and
#{\gamma^m=\gamma}, so the class is #{[-\omega,1]=[\omega,4]=h}.
For #{\lambda=\gamma^2}, the choice #{\nu=1} gives #{u=\omega}
and #{\gamma^m=\gamma^4}, which is #{h}. For #{\lambda=\gamma^4},
the choice #{\nu=-1} gives #{u=\omega^2} and
#{\gamma^m=\gamma^2}, which is #{h^2}. Each of these #{4}
classes is #{h} or #{h^2}. Together with the identity, the
stabilizer of #{v} has order three. The image #{g\cdot v}
depends only on the left coset of this stabilizer. Distinct
cosets give distinct images, and every image arises, so the
orbit has #{72/3=24} points.}

\p{Let #{H=\{1,h,h^2\}}. For #{g=[u,m]} the conjugate is
#{ghg^{-1}=[u\omega u^{-1},4]=[-u\omega u^{-1},1]}.
This equals #{h=[\omega,4]} exactly when #{u} commutes with
#{\omega}. The two phase writings of the conjugate are #{4} and
#{1}; those of the identity are #{0} and #{3}; those of #{h^2}
are #{2} and #{5}. Thus the conjugate equals neither the identity
nor #{h^2}. Commuting with #{\omega} is the same as commuting
with #{s=\qi+\qj+\qk}. For #{u=a+b\qi+c\qj+d\qk} the products
have real part #{- (b+c+d)} on both sides, and the coefficients
of #{\qi,\qj,\qk} are
##{us=(a+c-d,\,a-b+d,\,a+b-c),\qquad
 su=(a+d-c,\,a+b-d,\,a-b+c).}
Equality forces #{b=c=d}. A Hurwitz unit of this form has
#{a^2+3b^2=1}. The #{8} coordinate units contribute #{1} and
#{-1}. The half-integer units contribute the #{4} elements
#{\pm\omega,\pm\omega^2}. Thus #{6} Hurwitz units commute with
#{\omega}. Each extends by all #{6} powers of #{\gamma}, giving
#{36} product elements. The quotient identification has no fixed
point, because a phase is never congruent to itself plus #{3}
modulo #{6}. The centralizer of #{h} in #{\Gew} therefore has
order #{18}.}

\p{The map #{H\times H\to HgH} given by #{(x,y)\mapsto xgy} has
fibers of size #{|H\cap gHg^{-1}|}. Indeed
#{xgy=x'gy'} rearranges to an element of that intersection, and
each such element determines one pair #{(x',y')} from #{(x,y)}.
Hence #{|HgH|=\frac{9}{|H\cap gHg^{-1}|}}. A nontrivial element
of the intersection would force the conjugate of #{h} to lie in
#{H}. The phases exclude the identity and #{h^2}, so this happens
only when #{g} centralizes #{h}. The intersection then has order
#{3}, and otherwise order #{1}. The double coset therefore has
size #{3} or size #{9}. The centralizer is a subgroup of order
#{18} containing #{H}, and #{H} is central there, so its double
cosets are the #{6} cosets of #{H}. The remaining
#{72-18=54} elements form #{6} double cosets of size #{9}.}
}
